   180	### 2.10 `fault` — the tenth dimension (ratified in round 1)
   181	
   182	`none` · `backup_unwritable`.
   183	
   184	An injected environmental failure. It is not a property of the target, which
   185	is why it is not `live_type`; it is a genuine tenth axis, and it rode inside
   186	the `note` column as a prose directive until the round-1 debate ruled that a
   187	**dimension the harness parses out of prose is a dimension nothing
   188	validates**.
   189	
   190	Dropping those rows was the lower-friction alternative and was rejected:
   191	they are the backup-failure *safety* cells, and a failed backup followed by a
   192	delete is the data-loss path the whole backup-before-replace contract exists
   193	to prevent. A column is cheap; a hole is not.
   194	
   195	Consequently `note` now carries **prose only**. `indistinguishable=` and
   196	`open=` survive as annotations because neither changes what the fixture does
   197	or what the decision function returns.
   198	
   199	## 3. The verdict enum (draft — W1 ratifies)
   200	
   201	The outcome of a cell is a **pair**. Every defect found in the eleven review
   202	rounds was a cell whose pair was wrong — which is the evidence that the pair
   203	is the right shape for the answer.
   204	
   205	### 3.1 First field — what happens to the target
   206	
   207	| Verdict | Meaning |
   208	|---|---|
   209	| `DELIVER` | write the framework's version; the target had nothing |
   210	| `REFRESH` | replace existing content, **backup first** |
   211	| `PRESERVE_OWNED` | do not touch the target; **keep** the delivery record |
   212	| `PRESERVE_UNOWNED` | do not touch the target; do **not** record (the adopter owns it) |
   213	| `OMIT_RECORD` | target stays; its record leaves the manifest |
   214	| `ABORT_SURFACE` | refuse this surface, rc 0, named warning; the run continues |
   215	
   216	`ABORT_SURFACE` is distinct from `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`: it is the outcome when
   217	the framework *wanted* to act and could not do so safely — a failed backup,
   218	an unsupported special file — and the distinction matters because the
   219	operator must be told.
   220	
   221	### 3.2 Second field — where the manifest's digest comes from
   222	
   223	| `hash_source` | Meaning |
   224	|---|---|
   225	| `HASH_TARGET` | hash the bytes now on disk at the target |
   226	| `HASH_SOURCE` | hash the framework's copy in `$SOURCE_DIR` |
   227	| `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` | carry the digest the previous manifest recorded |
   228	| `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` | the computed hash of what the pointer heredoc *would* generate |
   229	| `HASH_NONE` | emit no record |
   230	| `LINK_RECORD` | emit `LINK  <relpath>  <target>` instead of a digest |
   231	
   232	The two fields are **orthogonal**. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_TARGET`
   233	records the adopter's edited bytes as the framework baseline — which is how
   234	a later upgrade comes to overwrite them and `uninstall.sh` comes to delete
   235	them. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` is the safe reading of the
   236	same intent. Nothing in the branch structure made that choice visible; a
   237	column does.
   238	
   239	### 3.3 What this replaces
   240	
   241	`FMS_HASH_ROOT_PATHS` and `FMS_LINK_PATHS` are per-path override lists added
   242	during the eleven rounds to narrow two global switches that turned out to be
   243	too wide. They are **special cases of an explicit `hash_source`**, and the
   244	implementation replaces them — it does not keep them alongside it. Adding a
   245	third override list is the failure mode this table exists to prevent.
   246	
   247	`FMS_PROTOCOL_HASH` carries **two different meanings** today: the canonical
   248	pointer hash on the upgrade path, and a *prior record digest* on the install
   249	continuity path. Under this model those are `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` and
   250	`HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` — distinct values that must not share a channel. See
   251	OQ-4.
   252	
   253	### 3.4 `HASH_TARGET` is the default, and it is never distinctly correct
   254	
   255	Filling the table surfaced something no single branch could show: across all
   256	61 rows, **`HASH_TARGET` is never the right answer.** It appears only as an
   257	`indistinguishable=` annotation on rows where the target was just written
   258	from the framework's own bytes, so the two candidates are equal by
   259	construction and the distinction is unobservable.
   260	
   261	Yet `HASH_TARGET` is precisely what the generator falls back to when no
   262	override is supplied. So the default is right only by coincidence — whenever
   263	target and source agree — and wrong in exactly the situation the override
   264	exists to handle: a preserved adopter edit. Three separate P1 defects are
   265	instances of that one sentence.
   266	
   267	This is an argument for making `hash_source` an explicit, required parameter
   268	of the verdict rather than a set of opt-in overrides on a permissive default.
   269	Recorded as evidence for the W1 debate, not decided here.
   270	
   271	## 4. Legality rules (pruning)
   272	
   273	A cell is **illegal** when the combination cannot occur against a real
   274	target. Every rule below removes cells; **each is named with its reason, and
   275	silent pruning is forbidden** — an unexplained absence from the TSV is
   276	indistinguishable from an oversight, which is how a defect class hides.
   277	
   278	| Rule | Statement | Reason |
   279	|---|---|---|
   280	| **R-01** | `operation=install_fresh` ⇒ `prior_record=none` | The manifest is written at the *end* of install. "Fresh" is defined as "no pre-existing manifest", so there is no prior testimony to read. |
   281	| **R-02** | `operation ∈ {install_fresh, install_rerun}` ⇒ `skip_requested=none` | `--skip` is an `upgrade.sh` flag. `install.sh` has no equivalent (verified: zero occurrences). |
   282	| **R-03** | `surface=protocol` ⇒ `source_has=yes` | The pointer is generated from a heredoc, never copied. There is no source file whose absence could be observed. |
   283	| **R-04** | `live_content=legacy_pristine` ⇒ `surface=spec` | The pristine fingerprints are a `SPEC/v1`-tree construct. No equivalent exists, or is needed, for a one-line marker or a generated pointer. |
   284	| **R-04b** | `live_content=degraded` ⇒ `surface=protocol` (PLAN-168 W2) | The degraded template is a pointer construct: only the generated `PROTOCOL.md` ever carried the `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}`-literal body. `SPEC/v1` legacy recognition already has `legacy_pristine`; the marker is a one-line version string with no template to degrade. |
   285	| **R-05** | `live_type=absent` ⇒ `live_content` undefined | Nothing to hash. |
   286	| **R-06** | `skip_requested=descendant` ⇒ `surface=spec` | Only `SPEC/v1` is a tree. A path *under* a single file cannot exist. |
   287	| **R-07** | `live_type=dir_empty` ⇒ `surface=spec` | For the single-file surfaces, an empty directory and a non-empty one behave identically (both yield no record and both are refused as non-regular). The distinction is only load-bearing where per-file records are emitted. |
   288	| **R-08** | `ceremony=user` ⇒ `surface ∈ {spec, protocol}` cannot yield `DELIVER` or `REFRESH` | WS4 guards forbid root surfaces under a user ceremony. **This prunes verdicts, not cells:** those surfaces still legally *appear* under `ceremony=user` as residue of a prior maintainer install, and those residue cells are exactly where two defects lived. |
   289	| **R-09** | `prior_record ∈ {link_match, link_retargeted}` ∧ `live_type ≠ symlink` ⇒ collapse to `link_retargeted` | `readlink` on a non-symlink yields empty, which never equals a recorded non-empty target. Keeping both would be two names for one observable state. |
   290	| **R-10** | Rows are **equivalence classes**, not raw tuples; a dimension the row's outcome does not depend on is written `*` | Forced, not preferred. The raw product is ~24,000 tuples; at the mandated per-cell timeout the suite could not run in a day, so it would not be run — and an unrun suite is worse than a smaller honest one. `*` is the harness's instruction to instantiate the canonical representative, and any dimension that turns out to matter must be split into explicit rows. |
   291	| **R-11** | `live_type=ancestor_symlink` ⇒ `surface ∈ {spec, marker}` | `PROTOCOL.md` sits at the target root, so between `$TARGET` and the leaf there is **no intermediate component** that could be a symlink. The guard is vacuous there, not missing. |
   292	
   293	### 4.2 Conventions carried in the TSV
   294	
   295	- `*` — don't-care, per R-10.
   296	- `-` — not applicable under a rule above (e.g. `live_content` when the
   297	  target is absent).
   298	- `note` may carry structured directives alongside prose:
   299	  `fault=<enum>` (an injected environmental failure),
   300	  `indistinguishable=<enum>` (two `hash_source` candidates that are equal by
   301	  construction on this row — the harness reports `AMBIG`, never a lucky
   302	  green), `invariant=<id>`, `open=<round-id>` (a defect this row asserts and
   303	  the current tree does not yet satisfy).
   304	
   305	### 4.1 Three draft rules, REJECTED with reason
   306	
   307	PLAN-167 §W0.1 offered five pruning rules as "already known". Three of them
   308	are wrong, and adopting them would have deleted cells that hold real
   309	defects. Recording the rejections here so they are not re-proposed.
   310	
   560	**`REFRESH` versus `PRESERVE` has no single sound signal.** When the incoming
   561	content equals what is already on disk, a refresh changes no bytes, and three
   562	candidate observables each fail somewhere:
   563	
   564	| Signal | Fails because |
   565	|---|---|
   566	| content digest | an identical-content refresh is invisible to it |
   567	| backup presence | the ADOPTER-FORK *preserve* path also snapshots — a backup proves the framework **looked**, not that it wrote |
   568	| modification time | `install.sh` re-runs placeholder substitution every invocation, rewriting the pointer with identical bytes and a fresh mtime — a write with no semantic content |
   569	
   570	The harness uses mtime, and **only on the upgrade path**, where the forced
   571	route is the one thing that rewrites these surfaces. The boundary is stated
   572	rather than assumed, because each of these signals looked universal until a
   573	row proved otherwise — and two of them were adopted, and withdrawn, during
   574	this work.
   575	
   576	The general lesson is worth more than the mechanism: in a space this shape,
   577	**the instrument needs the same adversarial scrutiny as the subject.** Five
   578	separate fixture defects were found while building this baseline, four of
   579	which produced a confident green or red that meant nothing.
   580	
   581	### 5.7 A guard placed on the right route, reached too late
   582	
   583	The `special`-file rows do not fail where they were expected to. The upgrade
   584	never reaches the SPEC or marker route at all: an earlier stage —
   585	`_emit_deprecation_warnings`, which shells out to a scanner that walks the
   586	whole target tree — opens the FIFO and blocks forever. The run dies before
   587	any surface is refreshed.
   588	
   589	Verified in isolation with both controls: the scanner alone, given a target
   590	whose only anomaly is a FIFO at the marker path, hangs indefinitely; the same
   591	scanner on the same tree with a regular file there exits 0.
   592	
   593	Two consequences, and the second is the more important one:
   594	
   595	1. It is a **new defect of a class the eleven rounds did not reach**. The
   596	   special-file guards were placed on the three routes that *own* the
   597	   surfaces. A tree-walking reader that runs earlier needs the same guard,
   598	   and does not have it.
   599	2. It means the existing guards are **masked**: no end-to-end run can
   600	   demonstrate that the FIFO branch of `_refresh_spec_contract` works,
   601	   because control never arrives there. A green suite would have proven
   602	   nothing about them. The table found this by asserting an outcome the
   603	   route was believed to produce and observing that the process died first.
   604	
   605	Per PLAN-167 §6 rule 2 this is recorded, not patched branch-locally. Per
   606	rule 5 the family sweep it implies is broader than the three surfaces: every
   607	tree-walking reader invoked during an upgrade is in scope.
   608	
   609	### 5.8 A continuity branch that cannot fire
   610	
   611	The symlinked-ancestor guard on `spec` and `marker` ends with a line that
   612	carries ownership forward when the pre-upgrade baseline holds a record. That
   613	line is **unreachable**.
   614	
   615	The relpath sanitizer rejects any record whose path traverses a symlinked
   616	component, and it runs when the manifest is *loaded* — before
   617	`_baseline_has_spec_record` is ever consulted. In the one situation the
   618	continuity line was written for, the record it looks for has already been
   619	removed from the sanitized manifest. The observable outcome is
   620	`OMIT_RECORD`, never the intended `PRESERVE_OWNED`.
   621	
   622	The table therefore expects `OMIT_RECORD` here — but the fix is **not** to
   623	make the branch fire. Making it fire would mean honouring a record whose
   624	path crosses a symlink, which is exactly the provenance fence ADR-155
   625	decision (v) exists to enforce. Under-claiming ownership is the recoverable
   626	direction (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3). The branch should be **deleted as dead
   627	code**, because a line that promises something it cannot deliver is worse
   628	than no line: it reads, to the next maintainer, like a guarantee.
   629	
   630	## 6. Open questions — the W1 debate agenda
   631	
   632	These are the points where the three input authorities — the eleven review
   633	verdicts, the live branch, and ADR-155/AMEND-1 — do **not** agree. PLAN-167
   634	§W0.1 requires that they be recorded rather than resolved unilaterally.
   635	
   636	- **OQ-1 — Is `ABORT_SURFACE` one verdict or two?**
   637	  A failed backup and an unsupported special file both leave the target
   638	  untouched with a named warning, but they differ in whether the framework
   639	  *could* have proceeded. If the manifest outcome is identical, the enum
   640	  should merge them; if the operator's next action differs, it should not.
   641	
   642	- **OQ-2 — What is the `hash_source` of `ABORT_SURFACE` when a prior record
   643	  exists?** Refusing to touch a surface is not evidence about ownership. The
   644	  live code answers this differently on different branches.
   645	
   646	- **OQ-3 — Version reporting under an external-target `--pin` downgrade.**
   647	  Dropping the marker record makes readers fall back to a `VERSION` the
   648	  upgrade never writes on an external target. A truthful signal requires
   649	  deriving from the pinned source; that is a new mechanism, not a cell.
   650	
   651	- **OQ-4 — Splitting `FMS_PROTOCOL_HASH`.** It carries
   652	  `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` on one path and `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` on another.
   653	  Splitting is the model-faithful move; it also changes a canonical-guarded
   654	  signature.
   655	
   656	- **OQ-5 — Where does `_ownership_verdict()` live?**
   657	  A new library file is a new canonical path, which requires a
   658	  `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` entry and therefore a kernel ceremony. PLAN-167 §4
   659	  states a preference for the already-guarded
   660	  `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh` absent a reasoned veto. A veto
   661	  escalates to the Owner — it does not become an overnight kernel ceremony.
   662	
   663	- **OQ-6 — Does `install_rerun` need `--skip`?** R-02 prunes the whole
   664	  column for install today. If the answer is "install should honour skips
   665	  too", that is a feature decision, and the pruned cells become legal.
   666	
   667	- **OQ-7 — RESOLVED in round 1.** `fault` is now a real column (§2.10) and
   668	  `legacy_pristine_partial` a real `live_content` value. Both were prose
   669	  directives; both are now dimensions. The text below is kept as the record
   670	  of the question.
   671	
   672	  ~~**`fault` is a tenth axis the column contract does not have.**~~
   673	  Three defects are reachable only under an injected environmental failure
   674	  (an unwritable backup directory). That is not a property of the target, so
   675	  it is not `live_type`; it is a genuine tenth dimension. PLAN-167 §W0.2
   676	  froze the TSV at fourteen columns, so it currently rides in `note` as
   677	  `fault=<enum>`. This is **declared, not smuggled**: the debate should
   678	  either bless a fifteenth column or rule those rows out of scope. Leaving
   679	  it in free text is the one option that should not survive — a dimension
   680	  the harness parses out of prose is a dimension nothing validates.
   681	
   682	- **OQ-9 — Is `OMIT_RECORD` an independent verdict at all?**
   683	  *New evidence from the shadow run:* the `OWN-0030` divergence exists ONLY
   684	  because the two instruments answer different questions — the suite asks
   685	  "did a record disappear?" and the function asks "was there a trustworthy
   686	  prior record?". Both end with no record on disk. **If the two verdicts
   687	  collapse into one, that divergence stops existing**, which is a second,
   688	  independent argument for the collapse: a distinction that generates
   689	  disagreement while having no observable consequence is not carrying its
   690	  weight.
   691	  Filling the table, every `OMIT_RECORD` row turned out to be a row that would
   692	  read `PRESERVE_UNOWNED` if no prior record had existed. The two are not
   693	  separate outcomes: they are the same outcome observed at different values of
   694	  `prior_record`, which is already a column. If that holds under scrutiny, the
   695	  enum has five verdicts, not six — and a redundant enum member is a place for
   696	  two branches to disagree about which one applies.
   697	
   698	- **OQ-10 — `--on-conflict` is an eleventh dimension.**
   699	  Surfacing §5.4d's cell also surfaced its modifier: the live code branches on
   700	  `--on-conflict={refuse|theirs|backup}` for exactly that cell, and `theirs`
   701	  or `backup` invert the outcome from preserve to overwrite. The nine
   702	  dimensions cannot express it, so the table currently describes the default
   703	  (`refuse`) only. Either it becomes a dimension or the table states in one
   704	  line that it is default-scoped — silence is the option §4 forbids.
   705	
   706	- **OQ-8 — Does the table cover readers, or only writers?**
   707	  One defect was a *reader* trusting a delivered-then-edited marker and
   708	  therefore suppressing a real update. Its cure follows from the same pair,
   709	  but the observation contract in `test-ownership-table.sh` derives the
   710	  verdict from (target state, manifest) — the reader is a third observable.
   711	  Either the contract grows a third channel, or reader rules live in a
   712	  sibling table.
   713	
   714	## 7. How to change this table
   715	
   716	1. A new defect becomes **a row**, never a branch-local patch.
   717	2. A finding that does not fit as a row is a **gap in the model**: record it
   718	   and escalate. Do not reshape the finding to fit.
   719	3. Removing a combination requires a **named rule in §4 with its reason**.
   720	4. Changing a pair in the TSV without changing the reasoning here — or the
   580	  red-team-corpus, secret-patterns-exchange, etc.
   581	
   582	After install, `ls TARGET/SPEC/v1/*.md` should list all schemas.
   583	The schemas are **read-only contracts** — adopters that need to
   584	customize behavior do so via `settings.json` env-var overrides
   585	(documented in `docs/GOVERNANCE.md`), never by editing the schema
   586	files. A version bump in `VERSION` carries the SemVer guarantee
   587	that minor/patch changes do NOT break the schemas; major bumps
   588	publish a new `SPEC/v2/` alongside.
   589	
   590	To verify what framework version a target is running:
   591	
   592	```bash
   593	cat TARGET/.claude/.framework-version   # preferred — refreshed on every upgrade
   594	# Example output: 1.3.0
   595	cat TARGET/VERSION                      # fallback (pre-v1.3.0 installs)
   596	```
   597	
   598	Prefer `.claude/.framework-version` as the forensic anchor when an
   599	adopter reports a bug. The root `VERSION` file matches the git tag of
   600	the source framework checkout **at install time only**: `upgrade.sh`
   601	deliberately never touches it (an adopter repo may have its own
   602	`VERSION`, and taking it over is the S238/ADR-155 clobber class — see
   603	`ADR-155-AMEND-1`), so on an upgraded install `VERSION` reports the
   604	ORIGINAL install version, not the current one. The marker is refreshed
   605	on every upgrade and is cross-checked against `VERSION` in every
   606	framework release; fall back to `VERSION` only on pre-v1.3.0 installs
   607	that have not upgraded yet.
   608	
   609	---
   610	
   611	## Upgrade flow
   612	
   613	To refresh framework-derived content in an existing adopter install
   614	(without touching user-customized files), use `scripts/upgrade.sh`:
   615	
   616	```bash
   617	cd /path/to/ceo-orchestration   # source framework checkout
   618	git pull                         # get the latest framework
   619	bash scripts/upgrade.sh /path/to/your/project --pin v1.3.0
   620	```
   621	
   622	What gets refreshed:
   623	
   624	- `.claude/team.md`, `.claude/frontend-team.md`
   625	- `.claude/skills/`, `.claude/hooks/`, `.claude/scripts/`,
   626	  `.claude/commands/`
   627	- `.claude/pitfalls-catalog.yaml`, `.claude/task-chains.yaml`
   628	- `PROTOCOL.md` pointer (skipped on `--ceremony user` installs — a user
   629	  install never creates root files)
   630	- `SPEC/v1/` — **forced route** (skipped on `--ceremony user` installs):
   631	  the SPEC is the published compliance contract, so a local edit is a
   632	  *fork of the contract*, not a customization — a framework-owned
   633	  `SPEC/v1` is backed up to `.claude.bak/<timestamp>/SPEC/v1` and
   634	  replaced wholesale. Ownership follows the recorded delivery (the
   635	  ADR-155 baseline manifest); a pre-existing `SPEC/v1` with no delivery
   636	  record is byte-compared against the pristine SPECs shipped at v1.2.0
   637	  and earlier — a match refreshes it, anything else is preserved in
   638	  place with a named WARNING (ADR-155-AMEND-1).
   639	- `.claude/.framework-version` — the framework version marker, rewritten
   640	  to the source version on every upgrade (this is what
   641	  `check-framework-updates.sh` and forensic triage read post-upgrade).
   642	
   643	What is **NOT** touched (user data):
   644	
   645	- `CLAUDE.md`, `MEMORY.md`
   646	- `.claude/agent-metrics.md`
   647	- `VERSION` (root) — **deliberately**: `install.sh` is skip-if-exists,
   648	  so on an adopter repo with its own `VERSION` the framework never
   649	  wrote there, and an upgrade overwrite would take the adopter's file
   650	  (the S238/ADR-155 class). The root `VERSION` is an install-time
   651	  snapshot forever; the current framework version lives in
   652	  `.claude/.framework-version`. Do not "fix" this asymmetry — see
   653	  `ADR-155-AMEND-1`.
   654	
   655	Ceremony on upgrade: `upgrade.sh` reads the recorded install ceremony
   656	from `.claude/.install-state.json` with a dedicated reader that runs
   657	even under `--no-replay`. **Installs without a readable
   658	`.install-state.json` (all pre-Wave-B installs) are treated as
   659	`maintainer` on upgrade** — that is the fail-open, pre-existing
   660	behavior; if your install was `--ceremony user` and predates the state
   661	file, re-run `install.sh --ceremony user` once so the ceremony is
   662	recorded before upgrading.
   663	
   664	`.claude/settings.json` is a special case since v1.2.0: `upgrade.sh` runs a
   665	3-state per-leaf-key **baseline migration** on it (e.g. the pair-rail
   666	registration-timeout cap 150 → 210 s, ADR-110-AMEND-2). A key is only
   667	changed when it still holds the old framework default — operator-chosen
   668	values are preserved — and the pre-migration file is copied to
   669	`.claude.bak/<timestamp>/` first.
   670	
   671	Run `bash scripts/upgrade.sh --help` for the full flag list. Key
   672	flags:
   673	
   674	- `--pin <tag>` — pin source to a specific tag/SHA (refuses if
   675	  target has uncommitted `.claude/` changes; the pin is the audit
   676	  anchor for what was actually installed).
   677	- `--dry-run` — preview what WOULD be replaced.
   678	- `--skip <glob>` — exclude files from the overwrite (repeatable).
   679	  Use this to protect adopter customizations to specific framework
   680	  scripts (e.g. `--skip='.claude/scripts/local/*'`).
   681	- `--no-diff-warn` — silence the F-CHAOS-3 "customization will be
   682	  replaced" warnings (default: warnings ON).
   683	
   684	A backup is automatically copied to `TARGET/.claude.bak/{timestamp}/`
   685	before any file is overwritten. If the upgrade aborts on error, the
   686	backup is preserved for forensic recovery.
   687	
