    20	
    21	## §1 What this amendment changes
    22	
    23	ADR-155 built the baseline-manifest engine on one shared enumeration
    24	(`scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`) and enumerated the root
    25	`PROTOCOL.md` **unconditionally** (decision (i)). The v1.3.0-rc.1 re-pass
    26	(PLAN-166 finding F3) showed that unconditional — or ceremony-only —
    27	enumeration is itself an ownership defect, and that `SPEC/v1` (shipped by
    28	`install.sh` since PLAN-087 but never enumerated, never refreshed) had
    29	become a stale, unwatched contract on every upgraded adopter. This
    30	amendment decides four things:
    31	
    32	1. **The delivery-record ownership rule (general).** Every CONDITIONAL
    33	   entry of the framework-owned enumeration — `PROTOCOL.md`, `SPEC/v1`
    34	   and the new `.claude/.framework-version` marker — derives from the
    35	   **registered delivery**, never from the ceremony alone and never from
    36	   file presence (§3).
    37	2. **`SPEC/v1` joins the upgrade surface via a FORCED route** — not the
    38	   generic `backup_and_replace` classified walk — with a deterministic
    39	   pristine-content migration for v1.2-and-earlier legacy installs (§4).
    40	3. **`.claude/.framework-version`** becomes a tracked file of the
    41	   framework repo, written explicitly on install (`install_one`,
    42	   skip-if-exists) and force-refreshed + read-back-validated on upgrade;
    43	   marker-first readers consult the SAME delivery record before trusting
    44	   it (§5).
    45	4. **The root `VERSION` file stays OUT of the upgrade surface and OUT of
    46	   the enumeration — permanently and deliberately** (§2). This section
    47	   exists so the next maintainer does not "fix" the asymmetry and reopen
    48	   the class.
    49	
    50	## §2 Why root VERSION is exempt — do not repair this asymmetry
    51	
    52	`install.sh`'s `install_one` is **skip-if-exists**: on an adopter repo
    53	that already carries its own `VERSION` (most real repos version
    54	themselves), the framework **never wrote that file**. Any upgrade-side
    55	refresh of `VERSION` — `backup_and_replace` or a forced route — would
    56	therefore TAKE an adopter-owned file. That is not hypothetical: it is the
    57	exact shape of the S238 acme data-loss ("the verified worst case" in
    58	ADR-155's own words), and the baseline classifier would *confirm* the
    59	clobber rather than prevent it, because the recorded baseline would hash
    60	the framework's value (trap C.5, documented inside
    61	`_framework_manifest_set.sh`).
    62	
    63	So the asymmetry is: **every other framework-derived surface refreshes on
    64	upgrade; `VERSION` does not, ever.** The consequence — the root `VERSION`
    65	of an upgraded adopter reports the ORIGINAL install version forever — is
    66	absorbed by the marker (§5) and named in `INSTALL.md` (the forensic-anchor
    67	section now prefers `.claude/.framework-version` with a `VERSION`
    68	fallback). A future maintainer who notices "upgrade refreshes the marker
    69	but not VERSION — inconsistent!" is looking at a decided invariant, not an
    70	oversight. Reopening it requires amending THIS amendment.
    71	
    72	Inside the framework repo itself nothing changes: every framework-repo
    73	gate (`check-canonical-doc-freshness.py`, `verify-counts.sh`,
    74	`check_tier_a_spec_version_drift`) keeps reading `VERSION` as the
    75	authority. The marker-first preference is exclusive to readers operating
    76	on an ADOPTER tree — today, `.claude/scripts/check-framework-updates.sh`
    77	(without it, the checker re-reads the stale root `VERSION` post-upgrade,
    78	exits `behind-minor` and demands the same upgrade in an eternal loop —
    79	r8). `check_tier_a_npm_version_match` deliberately does NOT adopt the
    80	marker: in an adopter tree the root `package.json` is the APP's, and
    81	comparing the framework marker against the app version would be a
    82	permanent false-red; that check keeps its VERSION×package.json semantics
    83	(or skips when VERSION is absent).
    84	
    85	## §3 The delivery-record ownership rule
    86	
    87	**"Delivered" means REGISTERED ACTUAL DELIVERY, not ceremony (r17), and
    88	not file presence (r7/r13):**
    89	
    90	- A `--ceremony user` install SKIPS `install_spec_v1`,
    91	  `install_version` and `install_protocol_pointer` (WS4 guards). If the
    92	  enumeration emitted those paths unconditionally,
    93	  `write_install_manifest` would hash the ADOPTER's own `SPEC/v1` or root
    94	  `PROTOCOL.md` as framework-owned — and a later `uninstall.sh` (which
    95	  removes manifest-recorded, hash-matching files) could DELETE the
    96	  adopter's files (r7/r13).
    97	- Ceremony-conditional enumeration is still not enough: on a
    98	  `maintainer` install where the destination ALREADY had its own
    99	  `SPEC/v1`, `install_one` EXISTS-skips — the file on disk is the
   100	  adopter's, under a maintainer ceremony (r17).
   101	
   102	Mechanics (both writers, one reader):
   103	
   104	- `install.sh` flips `_DELIVERED_{SPEC,PROTOCOL,MARKER}` only where the
   105	  write ACTUALLY happened (`install_one` reports COPIED/LINKED via
   106	  `INSTALL_ONE_WROTE`; the pointer heredoc sets the flag on its own write
   107	  path, unreachable from the pre-existing early-return), journals a
   108	  `delivered_*` op into `.install-state.json`, and exports the flags as
   109	  `FMS_DELIVERED_*` to the shared enumeration.
   110	- The **baseline manifest** (`.claude/.install-manifest.sha256`) is
   111	  thereby the persistent delivery record: it carries records for the
   112	  three conditional paths **iff** they were delivered.
   113	- `upgrade.sh` resolves prior ownership from the pre-upgrade baseline
   114	  records (`_baseline_has_spec_record` / `_baseline_has_marker_record` /
   115	  the existing `_baseline_lookup "PROTOCOL.md"`), refreshes what it owns,
   116	  and re-exports the flags for the post-upgrade C.7 rewrite.
   117	- `doctor.sh` resolves the SAME flags from the sanitized baseline —
   118	  never from ceremony — before its orphan-scan enumeration: only-ceremony
   119	  would re-include paths a user install skipped and `--strict-orphans`
   120	  would flag the adopter's own files as orphans (r19); a blanket
   121	  maintainer default would do the same, and a blanket user default would
   122	  hide a delivered SPEC from a maintainer (r9 P2).
   123	- The enumeration's fail direction is pinned: an unset flag means NOT
   124	  enumerated. **Under-claiming ownership is recoverable (a file goes
   125	  unwatched); over-claiming is the delete-the-adopter's-file class.**
   126	
   127	The upgrade-side ceremony read is **replay-independent** (r9):
   128	`upgrade.sh --no-replay` sets `REPLAY=0` and skips
   129	`_read_install_state_request` entirely, so a ceremony that rode the
   130	replay would silently revert a user install to maintainer under the
   131	documented `--no-replay` flag. A dedicated `_read_install_state_ceremony`
   132	reader always runs, validates against the closed enum
   133	`{maintainer, user}`, and **fails open to `maintainer`** when the state
   134	is absent/unreadable (all pre-Wave-B installs) — the pre-existing
   135	behavior, named as a consequence in `INSTALL.md`. The same read gates
   136	`_refresh_protocol_pointer`, which previously ran unconditionally and
   137	`cat >`-created a root `PROTOCOL.md` that a user install deliberately
   138	never has (the latent bug the PLAN-166 F4 tree-comparison e2e exposes).
   139	
   140	## §4 SPEC/v1: forced route + pristine-content legacy migration
   141	
   142	The generic route cannot carry the SPEC. For a directory target with a
   143	baseline, `backup_and_replace` runs the per-file classified walk — which
   144	PRESERVES adopter edits. From the **second** upgrade on (baseline then
   145	contains SPEC records), an edited SPEC would classify ADOPTER-CUSTOMIZED
   146	and the stale-contract failure would return (r6). The declared semantics
   147	(OQ-3): `SPEC/v1` is the published compliance CONTRACT — an adopter edit
   148	is a **fork of the contract**, not a customization. Three-way merge is
   149	complexity without a consumer; refuse-and-instruct would block every
   150	upgrade that ships a SPEC change. Hence `_refresh_spec_contract`:
   151	framework-owned ⇒ backup whole tree to `.claude.bak/<ts>/SPEC/v1` +
   152	replace wholesale; user-ceremony installs never receive it.
   153	
   154	**Legacy migration (r20).** v1.2-and-earlier installs have NO delivery
   155	record for SPEC (the enumeration never included it), so
   156	framework-installed and adopter-authored `SPEC/v1` are indistinguishable
   157	by record. The ambiguity resolves by CONTENT: the target tree's
   158	fingerprint (sha256 over the `LC_ALL=C`-sorted `"<sha256(file)>  <relpath>"`
   159	lines of every file under `SPEC/v1`) is compared against the PRISTINE
   160	fingerprints of every SPEC/v1 the framework shipped at **v1.2.0 and
   161	earlier** — nine tags, three distinct trees, derived deterministically
   162	from pinned tag content (`git ls-tree` + `git show`; the derivation
   163	command is embedded next to the constants in `upgrade.sh`):
   164	
   165	| pristine fingerprint (sha256) | shipped by |
   166	|---|---|
   167	| `a4a4504a224d72a975a853dd71a75d8e678fef034a70deb49df291dbb712c161` | v1.0.0, v1.0.1, v1.0.1-rc.1 |
   168	| `94aa62f781285ce4897ad1220edf15e97b4e9d7b629f9f7ba3389da5d45f22b1` | v1.1.0, v1.1.0-rc.1 |
   169	| `469a49238867be181490214305b43bc7299f2bae3ef0b282a5452f6caf327f0b` | v1.2.0, v1.2.0-rc.1, v1.2.0-rc.2, v1.2.0-rc.3 |
   170	
   171	Match ⇒ framework-owned (byte-identical to a shipped release; the forced
   172	refresh loses nothing) ⇒ refresh + named NOTE. No match ⇒ ADOPTER-FORK ⇒
   173	**preserve in place** + snapshot to `.claude.bak/<ts>/SPEC/v1` + named
   174	WARNING with the hand-refresh instruction. A partial/unhashable tree
   175	never produces a fingerprint (fail toward preserve). Both legacy cases
   176	are fixtures; the pristine-match branch is additionally exercised
   177	end-to-end by the F4 install-v1.2.0→upgrade comparison job.
   178	
   179	## §5 The marker: forced+validated write, record-gated readers
   180	
   181	`.claude/.framework-version` is a **tracked file of the framework repo**
   182	(one line, byte-identical to `VERSION`) — not generated-only-at-destination,
   183	so the release protections are real and unconditional: the version bump
   184	writes it as its 12th site, `verify-counts.sh` cross-checks it against
   185	`VERSION` in every release, and `release.yml` asserts marker == VERSION
   186	fail-closed. In the enumeration it is a NORMAL file entry (the
   187	`FMS_HASH_ROOT` baseline rewrite preserves it with no special-case),
   188	conditional on delivery like the other two.
   189	
   190	Delivery is by **explicit writes on both paths** (the enumeration never
   191	delivers — it only records; r7): `install_one ".claude/.framework-version"`
   192	on install (skip-if-exists ⇒ a pre-existing adopter marker is NOT
   193	delivered), and a **forced + read-back-validated** rewrite on upgrade
   194	(differing pre-existing copy backed up first; a write that fails
   195	validation is NOT recorded as delivered). It lives inside `.claude/`, so
   196	both ceremonies receive it (the WS4 guard only forbids root files) and it
   197	is committable like the rest of `.claude/`.
   198	
   199	**Every marker-first reader consults the SAME record** (r20):
   200	`check-framework-updates.sh` trusts the marker only when the baseline
   201	manifest carries its delivery record, else falls back to `VERSION` — on a
   202	target where the marker pre-existed and was skipped, an unconditional
   203	read would report a stale version in a loop.
   204	
   205	## §6 Enforcement
   206	
   207	- `scripts/tests/test-upgrade-spec-ownership.sh` — record-owned forced
   208	  refresh with backup (the 2nd-upgrade scenario), user-ceremony +
   209	  `--no-replay` skip, legacy adopter-fork preserve, marker delivery +
   210	  pre-existing-marker fallback, doctor orphan-scan in both modes,
   211	  update-checker no-loop regression (AC-3).
   212	- The PLAN-166 F4 e2e (`smoke-install.yml`) compares install-built vs
   213	  upgrade-built trees per ceremony mode; its historical leg
   214	  (install v1.2.0 → upgrade) exercises the pristine-match migration.
   215	- `_framework_manifest_set.sh`, `install.sh`, `upgrade.sh` remain
   216	  `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` surfaces; this amendment's edits land only via the
   217	  PLAN-166 W1 Owner-GPG ceremony.
   218	
   219	## Consequences
   220	
   221	- **(+)** A `--ceremony user` install can never have its own `SPEC/v1`,
   222	  root `PROTOCOL.md` or marker inventoried as framework-owned — closing
   223	  the uninstall-deletes-adopter-files corridor (r7/r13/r17).
   224	- **(+)** Upgraded adopters get a fresh SPEC contract every upgrade, with
   225	  fork preservation and a deterministic legacy migration.
   226	- **(+)** Post-upgrade version reporting is truthful (marker), without
   227	  ever touching the adopter's root `VERSION`.
   228	- **(−)** Pre-Wave-B installs (no `.install-state.json`) are treated as
   229	  `maintainer` on upgrade — fail-open, named in `INSTALL.md`; a user-mode
   230	  pre-Wave-B adopter must re-run `install.sh --ceremony user` once to
   231	  record the ceremony.
   232	- **(−)** The delivery record inherits the baseline manifest's trust
   233	  class: target-side, UNSIGNED, advisory (ADR-155 Consequences). A
   234	  tampered record can add/remove ownership — the fail direction on a
   235	  MISSING record is preserve/fallback (today's behavior), never a new
   236	  escalation.
   237	- **(~)** An adopter whose fork of `SPEC/v1` is byte-identical to a
   238	  shipped release is claimed as framework-owned by the legacy migration —
   239	  accepted: the forced refresh is content-preserving up to the shipped
   240	  bytes they already had.

